Host GS hook: forward the GIF packets to a host GS instead of the runtime's own GS

What it fixes: the runtime always drew with its own software GS. This adds an optional host GS
(ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/gs/ps2_host_gs.h, PS2Runtime::setHostGs) so a program can plug in
another GS (the DON port connects PCSX2's GS library). With a host installed, the GIF arbiter's
drain() forwards each packet (paths 1, 2, 3) to gif_transfer, byte for byte; GS::writeRegister
becomes an A+D packet on path 3 and GS::consumeLocalToHostBytes calls read_fifo; the native GIF
fast paths of PS2Runtime that wrote straight into the internal GS are skipped; vsync receives the
privileged registers at each VBlank; CSR.RESET (bit 9) now calls reset (the old code ignored it).
A small per-path scanner (GifHostScanner) still tracks SIGNAL, FINISH and LABEL, and the five
pseudo-registers 0x59..0x5C and 0x5F that the internal GS accepts as A+D writes, so the game's
waits on CSR keep working. All callbacks run on the GameThread (EE scheduler executor), with
optional thread_begin/thread_end. Without a host installed nothing changes.

How it was verified: tests/runtime/host_gs_hook_test.cpp (fake host; scanner compared with the
internal GS on the same stream at several chunk sizes, interleaved paths, forwarding on paths
1/2/3, null host, reset ordering, GIF DMA and VIF1 DMA DIRECT through PS2Memory). Built from a
tree with patches 0001-0003 plus this one: all checks passed. Not exercised: VU1 XGKICK, a real
PS2Runtime (needs a window), a real GS library.

Based on: original code; the CSR/SIGNAL/FINISH/LABEL semantics mirror this tree's gs_frontend.cpp.

